WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.848 --> 00:00:04.681
(soft violin ensemble begins)

00:00:15.703 --> 00:00:19.536
(soft violin music continues)

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:34.040
- Before we start I wanted
to thank the musicians

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:35.780
from The Yale School of Music.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.130
There you are,

00:00:37.130 --> 00:00:41.570
I wish we could do this...
(audience applauding)

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.990
I wish we could do this
at every press conference,

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:46.480
I'm not sure if you're available.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.550
We also have some over
at the police station.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:52.007
Dean Blocker, I think you
were gonna come up and say,

00:00:52.007 --> 00:00:52.840
there you are,

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.200
a few words about your program.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:56.033
- Thank you.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.333
Thank you Mayor Elicker,
and President Salovey.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.970
Our quartet today,

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:08.060
represents many, many,
many groups from Yale

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:10.073
that participate in the community.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:13.520
I can say for this quartet,

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.400
and for many others,

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:19.450
that they learn as much from
working in the community,

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:21.060
as they give.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.410
And that's the great
magic of what we do here.

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:27.020
For those of us who lived
here for a long time,

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:31.180
but for our students who live
here for four or five years.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:34.910
The Music in Schools Program at Yale,

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:37.680
with which these ladies are affiliated,

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.713
serves around 1200 students
in the public schools.

00:01:44.386 --> 00:01:47.527
And these young artists have soloed with

00:01:47.527 --> 00:01:51.550
the New York Phil, the Seoul
Phil, all around the world.

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:53.470
Can you imagine being a 10-year-old,

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:54.970
and studying with one of them?

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:59.520
But, what they would tell
you is they're enriched

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.870
by the lives of these young
people that they work with,

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:05.113
and that we work with in this community.

00:02:06.787 --> 00:02:08.780
So, Mayor Elicker, and
those of you who are

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:13.520
representing the New Haven
organizations that host us,

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.310
and help us enable these interactions

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:19.130
where we not only can bring hope,

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:22.030
but receive hope, we thank you.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.200
And we look forward to the years ahead,

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.180
where these programs grow,

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:27.160
and are enriched.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:30.720
And I want to especially
thank Christina Lear,

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:33.930
our cellist, Rihanna Heath, the violist,

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:37.530
and Kate Art, and Anna Lee, our violinist.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:39.760
It's a great privilege to be here,

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:41.960
and to open up this momentous occasion.

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:43.063
Thank you, Mr. Mayor.

00:02:44.162 --> 00:02:47.079
(group applauding)

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:53.253
- Thank you.

00:02:54.898 --> 00:02:56.890
And before we start, some remarks.

00:02:56.890 --> 00:02:59.830
Wanted to make some acknowledgments,

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:03.610
and I'm gonna try to do this with my eyes,

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:04.960
just looking around the room,

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:07.690
I see Adler Furlow, I see Alder Sabin,

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:08.860
Alder DeCola,

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:11.570
of course, President Walker-Myers is here.

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:12.760
Other Alders?

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:14.973
Alder Marks, good to see Alder Marks.

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:17.373
Any other Alders?

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:22.370
Senator Martin Looney is here.

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:26.563
State Representatives, or State
Senators, good to see you.

00:03:27.646 --> 00:03:30.200
I also noticed Dr. Brown,

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:34.073
Dr. Brown, from Gateway,
Dr. Bertolino from Southern.

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:38.710
And Garrett is here from the Chamber.

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:43.710
Will, from the Greater New
Haven Community Foundation.

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:47.700
I believe Dr. Camille,
may be here, as well.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.856
Many other people.

00:03:49.856 --> 00:03:51.830
I also like to thank all
of the people that are,

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:53.160
and acknowledge your presence,

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:55.040
that will call me later on,

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.242
telling me that I forgot to
acknowledge your presence.

00:03:57.242 --> 00:04:00.530
(group laughing)

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:02.880
Today's a historic moment.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:04.624
It's a historic moment.

00:04:04.624 --> 00:04:06.310
This is a really big deal.

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:09.050
Today, Yale President, Peter Salovey,

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:12.367
Board of Alders President,
Tyisha Walker-Myers,

00:04:12.367 --> 00:04:15.040
and I, are announcing
a historic commitment

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:18.270
between Yale University
and the City of New Haven.

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:20.240
That will see the University contribute

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:23.850
an additional $52 million
in voluntary payments

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:27.210
to the City of New Haven
over the next six years.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:29.810
And, partner with the
City in an effort to build

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:32.710
a more equitable New Haven for all.

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:34.613
This is the most significant commitment

00:04:34.613 --> 00:04:37.880
that Yale University has
ever made to the City.

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:39.760
And, I'm grateful for Peter Salovey.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:41.850
Your partnership, and
your team's partnership

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:42.730
in making this happen.

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:44.020
I'd like to turn it over to you

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:46.259
to make some remarks, thanks.

00:04:46.259 --> 00:04:51.259
- Thank you.
(group applauding)

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:56.900
- Well, thank you, Mayor Elicker,

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:59.590
I appreciate those warm remarks.

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:03.730
And it is so wonderful to see
so many of you here today.

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:07.110
I won't try to acknowledge
everybody, just a few.

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:09.593
First of all, I'm delighted
to be here with you, Mayor.

00:05:10.709 --> 00:05:11.950
And the Board of Alders,

00:05:11.950 --> 00:05:16.200
particularly, President
Tyisha Walker-Myers.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:19.090
State Senate President, Martin Looney.

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:22.160
And so many neighbors and
friends with us today.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:23.823
Just one shout out,

00:05:24.917 --> 00:05:27.080
and that is a special
friend, Henry Fernandez.

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:30.780
Who really helped shepherd this day,

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:33.559
this agreement forward, and
really appreciate your work.

00:05:33.559 --> 00:05:36.476
(group applauding)

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:43.070
Obviously, my home team,
I thank them every day,

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:46.400
for the hard work and
partnership that they provide

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:47.513
at Yale University.

00:05:49.232 --> 00:05:50.900
So, I'm a long-time resident of this city.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:52.383
41 years this year.

00:05:53.635 --> 00:05:58.013
And I've been faculty member
for about 36 years of those 41.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:01.313
And I'm just deeply grateful,

00:06:02.468 --> 00:06:07.220
that today we can be together
to honor, and deepen,

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:10.300
Yale's strong, enduring relationship

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:12.290
with the City of New Haven.

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:15.090
The new commitments that
we're announcing today,

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:17.700
are, been formed by
decades of collaboration

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:21.930
between members of Yale, and
the New Haven communities.

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:24.660
And it's particularly
meaningful that we are

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:27.170
launching these new
initiatives as we prepare

00:06:27.170 --> 00:06:29.813
to emerge from a pandemic.

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:33.653
At this critical inflection point,

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:38.930
Yale and New Haven are
making an empathic statement.

00:06:38.930 --> 00:06:42.283
Making an empathic
statement about our bond.

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:46.240
We're choosing to work
together to make the most

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:49.860
of this remarkable moment
in the City's history.

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:54.860
Like never before, New Haven
is poised for accelerated

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:57.973
expansion and inclusive growth.

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:01.620
The population is growing,

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:04.220
along with the City's promising innovation

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:06.990
and economic infrastructure.

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:10.250
From new developments,
such as 101 College Street,

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:12.720
to flourishing residential, commercial,

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:14.510
and research spaces.

00:07:14.510 --> 00:07:16.760
Our city is entering a new era.

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:19.993
An era that I haven't
seen in my 41 years here.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:22.610
And, I'm grateful to the Governor,

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:25.460
for the state's role in
encouraging and supporting

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:27.120
private investment here in New Haven,

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:29.660
projects like 101 College Street.

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:34.660
We have an extraordinary
opportunity to position New Haven

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:37.970
for sustained, inclusive development

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:39.403
across every neighborhood.

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:42.810
So as an anchor institution,

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:45.390
and the City's largest employer,

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:46.660
Yale is proud.

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:50.550
Proud to join New Haven, and
the State of Connecticut,

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:53.393
increasing investments in our home city.

00:07:54.650 --> 00:07:58.070
First, Yale is extending it's lead,

00:07:58.070 --> 00:08:00.610
as the institution that makes the largest

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:04.740
annual voluntarily contribution
of any U.S. college,

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:07.990
or university, to it's home city.

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:10.320
We're increasing Yale's
voluntary contribution,

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:14.430
as you heard, to New Haven, by 52 million

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:16.843
over the next six years,

00:08:17.843 --> 00:08:20.150
$52 million over the next six years.

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:24.130
This equates to over $135 million

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:28.380
in our total voluntary payments
to support, for example,

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:31.860
our libraries, the
education of our children,

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:34.810
and many vital city services.

00:08:34.810 --> 00:08:38.770
Combined with funding from
the state's new Pilot Program,

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:41.660
continued contributions
by the private sector.

00:08:41.660 --> 00:08:43.680
Federal support,

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:47.970
will make tremendous, positive
difference in this city.

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:48.880
Mentioning Pilot,

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:52.400
let me take a moment to
thank Senator Looney,

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:54.290
for his vision and creativity,

00:08:54.290 --> 00:08:57.765
in re-thinking the Pilot
Program in a way...

00:08:57.765 --> 00:08:58.789
(group applauding)

00:08:58.789 --> 00:09:00.660
that has greater impact for New Haven.

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:03.577
(group applauding)

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:11.920
So in addition to the increase
in our voluntary payment,

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:15.600
Yale will also gradually offset the loss

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:17.270
in tax revenues,

00:09:17.270 --> 00:09:21.360
if the University should take
properties off the tax roll,

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:25.470
to create facilities for
teaching and research.

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:27.100
We have an innovated strategy

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:31.430
that will allow Yale to
provide long-term benefits,

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:33.850
both to the University, and the City,

00:09:33.850 --> 00:09:35.160
through our growth,

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:38.410
while ensuring that New
Haven does not lose out

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:40.753
on tax revenue in the short term.

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:46.670
Although our financial
support is important,

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:51.490
Yale is committing more
than just dollars today.

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:54.090
We've all seen how
American cities have faced

00:09:54.090 --> 00:09:56.200
a host of challenges.

00:09:56.200 --> 00:10:00.133
Many of which were exasperated
by the COVID pandemic.

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:05.830
So, we're establishing a new
center for inclusive growth

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:08.620
to help address these problems.

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:11.640
The center will examine,
develop, and implement

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:15.330
new strategies for
growing the City's economy

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:19.550
to the benefit of all New Haven residents.

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:21.890
It will compliment work that is being done

00:10:21.890 --> 00:10:24.680
by the Community Foundation
for Greater New Haven,

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:27.320
by Conn Corps, Next Haven,

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:28.900
COLAB,

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:30.290
Black Business Alliance,

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:32.200
New Haven Innovation Collaborative,

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:37.200
and many other organizations
concerned about this challenge.

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:41.490
So, I've asked Professor Kerwin Charles,

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:45.400
the Indra K. Nooyi Dean of
the Yale School of Management,

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:48.600
to launch this effort,
and he is with us today.

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:49.727
There he is in the back.

00:10:49.727 --> 00:10:52.644
(group applauding)

00:10:57.609 --> 00:10:59.460
So he'll have the help
of an advisory committee

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:03.000
of University, city,
and community leaders.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:05.830
And we will bring together
Yale's scholarly strengths

00:11:05.830 --> 00:11:08.890
to bear on issues at our front door,

00:11:08.890 --> 00:11:11.300
at our city's front door.

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:13.600
The Center for Inclusive
Growth will demonstrate

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:18.600
the power of our partnership
with New Haven in action.

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:22.610
So of course, the center will be opening

00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:25.670
in a city that has been undergoing
a wonderful renaissance.

00:11:25.670 --> 00:11:28.010
Over the years, artistic,

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:31.330
cultural, and dining
destinations have emerged

00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:32.960
across our city,

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:34.640
generating economic development,

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:39.020
inviting more people to
discover our vibrant community.

00:11:39.020 --> 00:11:43.100
So, I'm delighted that
another element of our plan,

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:45.490
our partnership together,

00:11:45.490 --> 00:11:47.300
creates a new public space,

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:50.150
where friends and neighbors can gather.

00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:52.000
By converting High Street,

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:54.820
between Chapel and Elm Streets,

00:11:54.820 --> 00:11:57.210
into a pedestrian walkway,

00:11:57.210 --> 00:11:59.740
free of vehicle access,

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:02.010
will foster an attractive social hub

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:07.010
near iconic and city landmarks
that everyone can enjoy.

00:12:07.270 --> 00:12:11.100
Just to be clear, the City will
continue to own the street.

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:15.940
Yale will redevelop and design this space,

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:20.003
and fund the renovation of
it, into a pedestrian mall.

00:12:22.710 --> 00:12:25.310
So the new innovations, I'm sorry,

00:12:25.310 --> 00:12:27.960
the new investments we're launching,

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:30.520
represent a major step forward

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:32.830
in Yale's enduring
commitment to New Haven.

00:12:32.830 --> 00:12:34.430
At the same time,

00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:36.180
we will continue to support an array

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:38.720
of existing Yale programs,

00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:41.860
expected to total over an
additional $100 million

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:46.250
in contributions over the next six years.

00:12:46.250 --> 00:12:48.360
So for example, New Haven Promise,

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:50.970
which provides scholarships for the City's

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:52.600
public school students to attend

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:54.740
any university in Connecticut,

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:58.980
will continue to be an
investment focus for Yale.

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:00.960
The Yale University Home Buyer Program,

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:04.990
that helps our employees
purchase homes in the City,

00:13:04.990 --> 00:13:06.713
will continue to be supported.

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:10.840
New Haven Works, which offers
job training, and placement,

00:13:10.840 --> 00:13:15.010
and other services,
continues to be a focus.

00:13:15.010 --> 00:13:16.400
Each of these initiatives,

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:18.400
including contributions to

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:21.350
the Downtown Business
Improvement District,

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:24.620
cultural events, and other local causes,

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:26.410
helps create a stronger,

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:29.690
and more resilient community together.

00:13:29.690 --> 00:13:32.450
Community where we all can live,

00:13:32.450 --> 00:13:33.840
where we all can work,

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:36.650
where we can feel that partnership between

00:13:36.650 --> 00:13:40.193
the University and the City, itself.

00:13:43.370 --> 00:13:45.930
I believe that our actions can exemplify

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:48.000
how institutions of higher education

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:51.560
can continue to partner
with their home cities

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:53.180
to serve the common good.

00:13:53.180 --> 00:13:56.730
And I hope we serve as
an inspiration to others.

00:13:56.730 --> 00:14:00.040
I'm so grateful to join
you, join all of you.

00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:02.310
Particularly Mayor Elicker,

00:14:02.310 --> 00:14:05.150
in writing the next chapter in Yale,

00:14:05.150 --> 00:14:07.610
and New Haven's shared history.

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:11.174
And I can't wait to get to work on all

00:14:11.174 --> 00:14:13.740
that lays ahead of us.

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:15.610
Hand-in-hand,

00:14:15.610 --> 00:14:20.210
we will create an exciting,
and prosperous future

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:21.550
for everyone here,

00:14:21.550 --> 00:14:23.300
for everyone in our city,

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:26.430
and for those who will come after us.

00:14:26.430 --> 00:14:28.290
Thank you all for being here.

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:30.327
Mayor Elicker, thank you for your support.

00:14:30.327 --> 00:14:33.244
(group applauding)

00:14:42.871 --> 00:14:43.936
Thank you.

00:14:43.936 --> 00:14:46.853
(group applauding)

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:51.920
- Thank you, Peter, for your partnership.

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:55.270
And your leadership and commitment
to the City of New Haven.

00:14:55.270 --> 00:14:56.730
Peter and I have gotten to know each other

00:14:56.730 --> 00:14:58.810
quite well over the past two years.

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:00.230
And to think back on our first meeting,

00:15:00.230 --> 00:15:01.380
and how far we've come,

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:05.393
in our trust for each other,

00:15:06.348 --> 00:15:07.181
but, I think more importantly,

00:15:07.181 --> 00:15:09.207
the representation of the University

00:15:09.207 --> 00:15:12.400
and the City's trust in collaboration,

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:13.700
is a testament to you,

00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:14.810
you and your team's willingness

00:15:14.810 --> 00:15:18.010
to really work hard to get
this across the finish line.

00:15:18.010 --> 00:15:20.480
- Thank you, Mayor, and
absolutely feel the same way

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:21.909
about you and your team.

00:15:21.909 --> 00:15:23.137
- Thank you.

00:15:23.137 --> 00:15:25.510
I'd also like to thank both teams

00:15:25.510 --> 00:15:27.143
that have made this possible.

00:15:29.385 --> 00:15:30.780
To the Yale team,

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:33.843
you have worked tirelessly

00:15:33.843 --> 00:15:36.900
to come to the table with openness,

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:38.250
and honesty.

00:15:38.250 --> 00:15:39.840
And to our city team,

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:41.890
Michael Gormany, Kevin Alvarez,

00:15:41.890 --> 00:15:44.230
Shawn Madison, Sue Weisselberg,

00:15:44.230 --> 00:15:45.740
a volunteer.

00:15:45.740 --> 00:15:46.823
Mike Piscitelli,

00:15:47.700 --> 00:15:49.840
thank you for expanding
your work to include

00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:52.030
this very important project.

00:15:52.030 --> 00:15:52.863
And to Henry,

00:15:55.190 --> 00:15:56.923
the lead on the City's team.

00:15:57.850 --> 00:16:00.490
Who has volunteered hours, and hours,

00:16:00.490 --> 00:16:03.770
of your time, your wisdom, your expertise,

00:16:03.770 --> 00:16:05.110
over this past year.

00:16:05.110 --> 00:16:08.740
Thank you for what you helped
us all accomplish together.

00:16:08.740 --> 00:16:10.240
I spoke with Peter last night,

00:16:10.240 --> 00:16:12.740
and we both felt that
it was very important

00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:15.320
to acknowledge the work
that you have done,

00:16:15.320 --> 00:16:19.340
and to ask you to share
some words with all of us.

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:20.471
Henry?

00:16:20.471 --> 00:16:23.388
(group applauding)

00:16:29.180 --> 00:16:30.883
- Thank you, thank you, Mayor.

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:34.530
My family thanks you as well,

00:16:34.530 --> 00:16:38.310
for our being able to get
their husband, and dad, back.

00:16:38.310 --> 00:16:41.210
After a year of this effort.

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:43.580
But it was wonderful.

00:16:43.580 --> 00:16:44.723
I do wanna give,

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:49.500
I wanna echo what the
mayor said about the teams

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:51.900
on both sides, here.

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:55.683
It was wonderful to work with all you.

00:16:56.870 --> 00:16:58.880
But I wanna give a special thanks

00:17:00.532 --> 00:17:01.810
to Jack Callahan, from Yale,

00:17:01.810 --> 00:17:03.100
who is a son of New Haven,

00:17:03.100 --> 00:17:05.570
and an honest negotiating
partner on the other side.

00:17:05.570 --> 00:17:06.796
Thank you, Jack.

00:17:06.796 --> 00:17:09.713
(group applauding)

00:17:12.290 --> 00:17:14.980
So, I wanna start by just talking

00:17:14.980 --> 00:17:16.703
a little bit about what we did.

00:17:18.806 --> 00:17:22.760
So, we spent time understanding
each other's positions.

00:17:22.760 --> 00:17:25.464
It was apparent that over the years,

00:17:25.464 --> 00:17:26.710
as people changed in offices,

00:17:26.710 --> 00:17:29.000
and new actors emerged,

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:32.370
both relationships, and understandings,

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:36.110
between the University and
the City, weren't present.

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:39.340
I don't mean that everyone become friends

00:17:39.340 --> 00:17:40.173
through this process,

00:17:40.173 --> 00:17:42.610
and that certainly was not our goal.

00:17:42.610 --> 00:17:45.380
But we were able to explain our positions

00:17:45.380 --> 00:17:48.750
and understand each other's
goals and interests.

00:17:48.750 --> 00:17:51.010
I think Yale came to understand

00:17:51.010 --> 00:17:54.580
that New Haven was addressing
structural problems,

00:17:54.580 --> 00:17:58.323
inherent in how state law treats cities,

00:17:59.256 --> 00:18:01.470
and taxes city residents.

00:18:01.470 --> 00:18:05.680
The City's team came to
understand how Yale sees it growth

00:18:05.680 --> 00:18:08.720
and how it is balancing competing demands

00:18:08.720 --> 00:18:10.523
within it's own institution.

00:18:11.780 --> 00:18:14.200
We spent some time as a group,

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:16.640
also discussing issues that are far bigger

00:18:16.640 --> 00:18:19.560
than either the City, or the University.

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:22.750
These issues include
matters of racial justice,

00:18:22.750 --> 00:18:26.760
and economic disparity that
have built up over centuries,

00:18:26.760 --> 00:18:29.540
in our city, and our country.

00:18:29.540 --> 00:18:31.960
And the recent ramifications
of these issues

00:18:31.960 --> 00:18:34.480
in the context of COVID-19,

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:35.910
climate change,

00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:38.210
and the protest movements around policing,

00:18:38.210 --> 00:18:40.033
across the country, and the globe.

00:18:41.020 --> 00:18:42.970
And while these issues are bigger

00:18:42.970 --> 00:18:44.690
than any one of us,

00:18:44.690 --> 00:18:47.420
or either the City, or the university,

00:18:47.420 --> 00:18:51.020
that doesn't mean that
we can ignore history.

00:18:51.020 --> 00:18:52.510
Or that we can ignore equity,

00:18:52.510 --> 00:18:54.300
or the disparities that are obvious

00:18:54.300 --> 00:18:55.703
for all of us in our city.

00:18:58.675 --> 00:19:00.847
As President Salovey mentioned,

00:19:00.847 --> 00:19:03.110
we saw to tackle three significant issues.

00:19:03.110 --> 00:19:04.720
First, is the amount of funding

00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:06.700
that goes from the University to the City

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:08.800
in voluntary payments.

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:11.590
I just wanna be really clear
about what we're talking about.

00:19:11.590 --> 00:19:13.460
In the first year,

00:19:13.460 --> 00:19:17.560
over $23 million will
go from the University,

00:19:17.560 --> 00:19:18.800
to the City.

00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:22.910
It will go up every year,
over the course of five years.

00:19:22.910 --> 00:19:24.580
And then it will drop in year six,

00:19:24.580 --> 00:19:27.490
which I suggest to the Mayor,

00:19:27.490 --> 00:19:29.440
would be a good time to start negotiating

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:30.728
before you get there.

00:19:30.728 --> 00:19:31.561
(group chuckling)

00:19:31.561 --> 00:19:32.840
Sorry!

00:19:32.840 --> 00:19:34.106
Now that we're done.

00:19:34.106 --> 00:19:35.620
(group laughing)

00:19:35.620 --> 00:19:37.690
There's a big red light in year six.

00:19:37.690 --> 00:19:38.690
Pay attention to it.

00:19:40.390 --> 00:19:42.810
As a result of this agreement,

00:19:42.810 --> 00:19:45.550
Yale now contributes more
than any other university

00:19:45.550 --> 00:19:47.290
to it's hometown.

00:19:47.290 --> 00:19:50.670
In the past, different
universities could quibble

00:19:50.670 --> 00:19:53.440
about whether they were paying more,

00:19:53.440 --> 00:19:56.740
or how to count one kind
of payment structure

00:19:56.740 --> 00:19:58.150
over another.

00:19:58.150 --> 00:20:01.380
There is no longer
anything to quibble about.

00:20:01.380 --> 00:20:03.540
Yale's annual contributions

00:20:03.540 --> 00:20:05.803
do not have anything close to them.

00:20:06.950 --> 00:20:08.880
I firmly believe, however,

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:11.900
that other universities should see this

00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:13.233
as the new standard.

00:20:14.330 --> 00:20:16.890
We are in the midst of a global pandemic,

00:20:16.890 --> 00:20:19.520
in a time of great economic disparity.

00:20:19.520 --> 00:20:21.670
Where anti-democratic forces

00:20:21.670 --> 00:20:24.320
use these tragedies to try to divide us.

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:27.340
In this moment, other
well-known universities

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:30.300
around the country, can
only truly be great,

00:20:30.300 --> 00:20:33.110
if they try to beat what we have done,

00:20:33.110 --> 00:20:35.240
and reset again the standard.

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:36.253
But for today,

00:20:37.390 --> 00:20:40.653
I feel good about the new
standard that we have set.

00:20:41.680 --> 00:20:45.050
Second, is the issue
of taxable properties.

00:20:45.050 --> 00:20:47.100
Connecticut cities have
historically relied on

00:20:47.100 --> 00:20:49.560
property taxes to pay their bills.

00:20:49.560 --> 00:20:53.220
Indeed, it has been the only
major tool that they control.

00:20:53.220 --> 00:20:56.920
This has created a
negative town down dynamic,

00:20:56.920 --> 00:20:59.630
between New Haven and Yale,

00:20:59.630 --> 00:21:01.073
basically forever.

00:21:02.930 --> 00:21:05.193
Because of the hard
work of Senator Looney,

00:21:06.574 --> 00:21:08.370
and the entire New Haven delegation,

00:21:08.370 --> 00:21:11.180
Tiered Pilot is now a reality.

00:21:11.180 --> 00:21:14.730
This new relationship
between the City and Yale

00:21:14.730 --> 00:21:18.900
is possible, in part,
because of Tiered Pilot.

00:21:18.900 --> 00:21:23.030
Specifically, Yale will
make payments to the City

00:21:23.030 --> 00:21:26.820
for any properties taken off the tax rolls

00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:30.597
at the full amount for three years,

00:21:30.597 --> 00:21:34.080
and then, in declining
amounts for another 10 years.

00:21:34.080 --> 00:21:35.950
Because of the combination of Pilot,

00:21:35.950 --> 00:21:37.350
and Yale's commitment,

00:21:37.350 --> 00:21:41.470
the City will now be whole,
for years after any conversion

00:21:41.470 --> 00:21:43.313
of property to tax exempt.

00:21:44.950 --> 00:21:47.560
That takes care of the
short and medium-term.

00:21:47.560 --> 00:21:48.580
And in the long term,

00:21:48.580 --> 00:21:51.060
we believe that growth at the University,

00:21:51.060 --> 00:21:53.040
for instance, in the sciences,

00:21:53.040 --> 00:21:56.580
will help create tax-paying
buildings for businesses,

00:21:56.580 --> 00:21:58.173
and good-paying jobs.

00:21:59.030 --> 00:22:02.130
Third, we recognize
the issues of disparity

00:22:02.130 --> 00:22:03.760
that I mentioned earlier.

00:22:03.760 --> 00:22:04.700
To tackle these,

00:22:04.700 --> 00:22:06.900
the University, and the
City, will work together

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:08.773
at The Center for Inclusive Growth.

00:22:09.680 --> 00:22:12.513
And as, the President and Mayor,

00:22:13.948 --> 00:22:15.840
what the President said,
and the Mayor will say,

00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:20.170
I'm excited for the leadership
of Dean Kerwin Charles.

00:22:20.170 --> 00:22:24.560
This center will not
simply study the problems.

00:22:24.560 --> 00:22:26.540
But instead, will use best research

00:22:26.540 --> 00:22:30.010
to facilitate the City and
the University's work together

00:22:30.010 --> 00:22:33.020
to address real issues
in our neighborhoods.

00:22:33.020 --> 00:22:35.350
Those issues that effect
low-income people,

00:22:35.350 --> 00:22:37.320
African-Americans, Latinos,

00:22:37.320 --> 00:22:41.160
immigrants and refugees
in our community the most.

00:22:41.160 --> 00:22:43.730
Including, climate change,

00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:46.300
access to high-speed internet.

00:22:46.300 --> 00:22:49.430
And if we recall over the last 18 months,

00:22:49.430 --> 00:22:51.360
we saw the lack of high-speed internet

00:22:51.360 --> 00:22:53.993
play out with our school
children dramatically.

00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:56.790
Also, jobs, affordable housing,

00:22:56.790 --> 00:22:58.930
and a myriad of other needs.

00:22:58.930 --> 00:23:02.200
The simple idea is that
we can build a city,

00:23:02.200 --> 00:23:03.303
and the people,

00:23:04.530 --> 00:23:05.653
the simple idea,

00:23:07.379 --> 00:23:08.520
is that we can build a city that grows

00:23:08.520 --> 00:23:10.830
and benefits both Yale University,

00:23:10.830 --> 00:23:12.810
in the center of our community,

00:23:12.810 --> 00:23:15.170
and the people across the
City of all backgrounds,

00:23:15.170 --> 00:23:18.020
in all neighborhoods, who love
the City and call it home.

00:23:18.860 --> 00:23:20.050
In five or six years,

00:23:20.050 --> 00:23:22.400
the City and Yale can re-evaluate.

00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:24.450
We probably got some things right.

00:23:24.450 --> 00:23:26.750
And may have gotten some things wrong.

00:23:26.750 --> 00:23:29.650
They may need to be more
aggressive in some areas,

00:23:29.650 --> 00:23:31.063
and back off in others.

00:23:32.410 --> 00:23:34.610
Now, I do wanna say a
word about the street.

00:23:36.210 --> 00:23:37.110
About High Street.

00:23:38.540 --> 00:23:41.220
Certainly Yale is interested
in having High Street

00:23:41.220 --> 00:23:44.163
operate as no longer a street for cars.

00:23:45.070 --> 00:23:49.330
But we agreed early-on that
we would do so differently,

00:23:49.330 --> 00:23:51.290
than done previously.

00:23:51.290 --> 00:23:53.883
High Street will remain
a public thoroughfare.

00:23:54.940 --> 00:23:56.983
Legally owned by the City of New Haven.

00:23:57.945 --> 00:24:00.440
Yales will design it, in
consultation with the City,

00:24:00.440 --> 00:24:04.480
specifically to ensure that is
a place for all New Haveners.

00:24:04.480 --> 00:24:07.060
Whether walking, or on bicycles,

00:24:07.060 --> 00:24:09.663
whether studying at Yale, or at Hillhouse.

00:24:10.590 --> 00:24:12.930
We look forward to a
beautiful new destination

00:24:12.930 --> 00:24:14.510
that welcomes all of us,

00:24:14.510 --> 00:24:16.160
and guests from around the world.

00:24:17.830 --> 00:24:19.780
Finally, having volunteered for this role,

00:24:19.780 --> 00:24:23.430
I ask only in return, that
the City and the University,

00:24:23.430 --> 00:24:25.960
see this agreement as a starting point.

00:24:25.960 --> 00:24:29.510
It can be the first step in a
new way of working together.

00:24:29.510 --> 00:24:33.260
Or, we can see it as a
series of transactions.

00:24:33.260 --> 00:24:36.470
It is, I believe, a fair
set of transactions.

00:24:36.470 --> 00:24:37.810
And in five years,

00:24:37.810 --> 00:24:40.940
the parties will need to get
together to negotiate again.

00:24:40.940 --> 00:24:42.750
But my hope is that we assembled,

00:24:42.750 --> 00:24:44.900
what we assembled here,

00:24:44.900 --> 00:24:47.900
shows that we can do much
bigger things together.

00:24:47.900 --> 00:24:48.950
That we can work together

00:24:48.950 --> 00:24:51.800
to ensure that Tiered
Pilot stays in place,

00:24:51.800 --> 00:24:54.810
and support Senator Looney in that.

00:24:54.810 --> 00:24:57.950
That we can do large, renewable
energy projects together,

00:24:57.950 --> 00:25:00.950
that make New Haven a
center of what's possible.

00:25:00.950 --> 00:25:03.910
That Yales students, and
students at Wexler Grant,

00:25:03.910 --> 00:25:06.880
can have ridiculously fast internet,

00:25:06.880 --> 00:25:10.383
and all of the opportunities
that come with that.

00:25:11.720 --> 00:25:14.170
Because we can work together
to cover our downtown,

00:25:14.170 --> 00:25:17.300
and our neighborhoods with fiber optics.

00:25:17.300 --> 00:25:19.140
That we set new standards for growth

00:25:19.140 --> 00:25:21.230
that focus on good-paying jobs,

00:25:21.230 --> 00:25:25.040
and allow our residents to
benefit equally from that growth.

00:25:25.040 --> 00:25:27.883
That we'll be aggressive in
re-building neighborhoods.

00:25:30.460 --> 00:25:33.620
And if we are able to tackle
these bigger things together,

00:25:33.620 --> 00:25:37.423
we can all rejoice when a new
building is built at Yale.

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:40.690
Just as much as we can rejoice,

00:25:40.690 --> 00:25:42.800
when more affordable housing is built

00:25:42.800 --> 00:25:45.460
in one our new residential high-rises,

00:25:45.460 --> 00:25:47.260
or one of our neighborhoods.

00:25:47.260 --> 00:25:52.097
Because cities are great only
when they benefit us all.

00:25:52.950 --> 00:25:56.823
Our city will be great when we
all look out for each other.

00:25:57.730 --> 00:26:02.090
And then, and when, those
with power and opportunity

00:26:02.090 --> 00:26:06.070
use it to make our city
more equal and just.

00:26:06.070 --> 00:26:08.570
With that, I'd like to say
thank you to the Mayor.

00:26:08.570 --> 00:26:11.591
I'll turn over the keys, you
can drive the car, I'm done.

00:26:11.591 --> 00:26:13.330
(group laughing)

00:26:13.330 --> 00:26:15.427
But thank you, thank
you for the opportunity.

00:26:15.427 --> 00:26:18.344
(group applauding)

00:26:25.480 --> 00:26:26.743
- Thank you again, Henry.

00:26:27.850 --> 00:26:30.423
This is real win for New Haven.

00:26:32.060 --> 00:26:33.180
The New Haven community,

00:26:33.180 --> 00:26:34.760
and it's a win for Yale University.

00:26:34.760 --> 00:26:37.460
Because what's good for New
Haven, is good for Yale.

00:26:37.460 --> 00:26:40.070
For years, so many people have contributed

00:26:40.070 --> 00:26:42.670
to making this moment a possibility.

00:26:42.670 --> 00:26:44.210
New Haven Rising, Unite Here,

00:26:44.210 --> 00:26:46.440
the Board of Alders, our state delegation,

00:26:46.440 --> 00:26:47.310
faith leaders,

00:26:47.310 --> 00:26:49.800
residents who have
attended public meetings,

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:51.320
people who have talked about it,

00:26:51.320 --> 00:26:53.090
emailed about it, written about it,

00:26:53.090 --> 00:26:55.140
posted on social media about it.

00:26:55.140 --> 00:26:58.060
This moment is because of your work.

00:26:58.060 --> 00:27:00.533
Without you, we would not be here today.

00:27:02.035 --> 00:27:03.840
And President Salovey and Henry went over

00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:05.780
the four points of the commitment.

00:27:05.780 --> 00:27:07.130
And I wanna talk a little bit about

00:27:07.130 --> 00:27:11.870
what these points actually
mean for the City and Yale.

00:27:11.870 --> 00:27:13.430
First, the increase in voluntary payments

00:27:13.430 --> 00:27:15.280
from the University will amount to,

00:27:15.280 --> 00:27:20.240
over, as Peter said, $135
million over the next six years.

00:27:20.240 --> 00:27:21.550
To put this in perspective,

00:27:21.550 --> 00:27:23.180
Yale will be contributing to the City,

00:27:23.180 --> 00:27:24.483
more over the next six years,

00:27:24.483 --> 00:27:26.863
than it has in the past 20 years.

00:27:27.740 --> 00:27:29.320
At a time when the City has been facing

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:31.860
significant financial challenges,

00:27:31.860 --> 00:27:33.690
the University has now acknowledged

00:27:33.690 --> 00:27:36.463
that we need to partner
together to address them.

00:27:37.610 --> 00:27:39.500
Yale's increased contribution combined

00:27:39.500 --> 00:27:41.410
with the increased Pilot payments,

00:27:41.410 --> 00:27:43.870
championed by our state delegation,

00:27:43.870 --> 00:27:45.240
and Senator Looney,

00:27:45.240 --> 00:27:47.180
means that this fiscal year,

00:27:47.180 --> 00:27:50.550
the City will have received
a total of 59 million

00:27:50.550 --> 00:27:51.937
in additional contributions,

00:27:51.937 --> 00:27:55.240
for over 10% of our overall budget.

00:27:55.240 --> 00:27:56.440
That's truly remarkable.

00:27:57.800 --> 00:28:00.520
And it's an acknowledgement
that all parties,

00:28:00.520 --> 00:28:04.040
when we work together, can
accomplish incredible things.

00:28:04.040 --> 00:28:06.410
Second, Yale's voluntary
offset of the loss

00:28:06.410 --> 00:28:08.270
of tax revenues, when they take a property

00:28:08.270 --> 00:28:09.800
off the tax rolls.

00:28:09.800 --> 00:28:13.100
For years, Yale's growth
has taken taxable property

00:28:13.100 --> 00:28:14.240
off the grand list,

00:28:14.240 --> 00:28:17.380
putting pressure on the
City's budget, and taxpayers.

00:28:17.380 --> 00:28:19.510
It's created a dynamic where many felt

00:28:19.510 --> 00:28:22.610
that Yale's growth wasn't
benefiting our city.

00:28:22.610 --> 00:28:24.640
This new, innovative approach,

00:28:24.640 --> 00:28:27.310
better aligns the City,
and Yale's interest.

00:28:27.310 --> 00:28:29.790
It means that Yale's
growth benefits our city,

00:28:29.790 --> 00:28:33.080
not just through more jobs
and economic activity,

00:28:33.080 --> 00:28:36.660
but also, by maintaining a tax revenue.

00:28:36.660 --> 00:28:39.363
Now, when Yale grows, we all benefit.

00:28:40.420 --> 00:28:43.040
Third, the block of High
Street, between Elm and Chapel.

00:28:43.040 --> 00:28:45.770
In the past, the City
has sold public assets

00:28:45.770 --> 00:28:47.260
to the University.

00:28:47.260 --> 00:28:50.300
In this commitment, we
worked to accomplish the goal

00:28:50.300 --> 00:28:53.100
of making the block of High Street safer

00:28:53.100 --> 00:28:56.090
for Yale constituents,
and all community members,

00:28:56.090 --> 00:28:58.910
in a way that ensures the
street remains a public asset

00:28:58.910 --> 00:29:00.380
for all to enjoy.

00:29:00.380 --> 00:29:03.020
Not only do we ensure
that property remains

00:29:03.020 --> 00:29:03.950
in the public domain,

00:29:03.950 --> 00:29:06.000
but together we set the right tone,

00:29:06.000 --> 00:29:08.230
that Yale is a part of our community.

00:29:08.230 --> 00:29:10.610
And finally, The Center
for Inclusive Growth.

00:29:10.610 --> 00:29:13.200
While each component of the
commitment announced today,

00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:14.330
is important,

00:29:14.330 --> 00:29:16.690
I believe the creation of this center,

00:29:16.690 --> 00:29:17.750
in particular,

00:29:17.750 --> 00:29:20.520
meets this moment in time, in history.

00:29:20.520 --> 00:29:23.190
When we started the
conversation with Yale,

00:29:23.190 --> 00:29:25.590
it wasn't just about writing a check.

00:29:25.590 --> 00:29:27.740
It was about how we work together

00:29:27.740 --> 00:29:28.660
to change our city,

00:29:28.660 --> 00:29:31.300
so that opportunities experienced by some

00:29:31.300 --> 00:29:32.410
in our community,

00:29:32.410 --> 00:29:34.660
could be experienced by all.

00:29:34.660 --> 00:29:38.507
This is a challenge our
world is struggling with.

00:29:38.507 --> 00:29:40.660
And Yale has decided to do this work

00:29:40.660 --> 00:29:42.320
in it's own backyard.

00:29:42.320 --> 00:29:44.320
In it's home, New Haven.

00:29:44.320 --> 00:29:45.970
Yale's enthusiasm for the Center,

00:29:45.970 --> 00:29:47.680
is key to it's success.

00:29:47.680 --> 00:29:49.460
President Salovey has been serious,

00:29:49.460 --> 00:29:51.590
not just about this in words,

00:29:51.590 --> 00:29:53.700
but in who he has asked to take it on,

00:29:53.700 --> 00:29:56.580
with Dean Charles, who
has specific expertise

00:29:56.580 --> 00:30:00.010
in inter-generational
transmission of economic status,

00:30:00.010 --> 00:30:02.212
and wealth inequality.

00:30:02.212 --> 00:30:03.180
Did I get that right?

00:30:03.180 --> 00:30:05.240
Mostly? Mostly?
(group chuckling)

00:30:05.240 --> 00:30:07.410
And the fact that Yale is contributing

00:30:07.410 --> 00:30:11.230
an additional $5 million
to help start the Center.

00:30:11.230 --> 00:30:13.190
We all know that New Haven is growing.

00:30:13.190 --> 00:30:15.430
And while our team at the City,

00:30:15.430 --> 00:30:18.600
works very hard to ensure
that growth is equitable,

00:30:18.600 --> 00:30:20.500
still there is much work ahead of us,

00:30:20.500 --> 00:30:23.500
so the communities, particularly
the communities of color,

00:30:23.500 --> 00:30:25.500
who have historically
been under-resourced,

00:30:25.500 --> 00:30:27.400
will benefit from this growth.

00:30:27.400 --> 00:30:29.500
The Center for Inclusive
Growth will set the tone

00:30:29.500 --> 00:30:32.780
that the University and
the City work together

00:30:32.780 --> 00:30:34.720
to ensure that everyone in New Haven

00:30:34.720 --> 00:30:36.680
has the opportunity to thrive.

00:30:36.680 --> 00:30:38.760
There are vast opportunities here,

00:30:38.760 --> 00:30:41.530
for harnessing both the real,
and intellectual capital

00:30:41.530 --> 00:30:43.170
of the University through the Center,

00:30:43.170 --> 00:30:45.210
and expanding it's impact
through partnerships

00:30:45.210 --> 00:30:47.700
with the Community Foundation
for Greater New Haven,

00:30:47.700 --> 00:30:49.420
other local colleges and universities,

00:30:49.420 --> 00:30:51.570
and other partners in the region.

00:30:51.570 --> 00:30:53.350
Taking together today's commitments

00:30:53.350 --> 00:30:55.810
fundamentally reset the relationship

00:30:55.810 --> 00:30:58.250
between Yale and the City of New Haven.

00:30:58.250 --> 00:30:59.220
And they put the City on

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:01.440
a more sustainable financial path.

00:31:01.440 --> 00:31:03.940
And they acknowledge that
the University and city,

00:31:03.940 --> 00:31:06.670
and committing to ensuring that everyone

00:31:06.670 --> 00:31:09.003
in our city has the opportunity to thrive.

00:31:09.870 --> 00:31:11.560
This would not have been possible

00:31:11.560 --> 00:31:16.200
without the partnership and
long work of years and years,

00:31:16.200 --> 00:31:19.280
by President Tyisha Walker-Myers.

00:31:19.280 --> 00:31:21.081
And I invite her now to speak.

00:31:21.081 --> 00:31:23.998
(group applauding)

00:31:30.510 --> 00:31:32.420
- Thank you, good afternoon.

00:31:32.420 --> 00:31:35.560
I wanna thank the Mayor
and President Salovey,

00:31:35.560 --> 00:31:38.770
and Mr. Fernandez, for laying
out the details of the deal,

00:31:38.770 --> 00:31:40.179
because now I don't have to do that.

00:31:40.179 --> 00:31:41.680
(group laughing)

00:31:41.680 --> 00:31:43.990
But I think it's really
important that I'm here

00:31:43.990 --> 00:31:46.750
to speak for the thousands of residents

00:31:47.968 --> 00:31:50.910
that work with the unions
and elected officials

00:31:50.910 --> 00:31:52.500
that brought awareness to the things

00:31:52.500 --> 00:31:53.900
that we need in New Haven.

00:31:53.900 --> 00:31:56.290
That never took their foot off the gas.

00:31:56.290 --> 00:31:58.900
That continue to force the conversations.

00:31:58.900 --> 00:32:01.960
And the greatest part of the
agreement, today, for me,

00:32:01.960 --> 00:32:04.690
is that those residents finally,

00:32:04.690 --> 00:32:07.040
finally have an opportunity to see that

00:32:07.040 --> 00:32:09.900
Yale actually heard what they were saying.

00:32:09.900 --> 00:32:11.990
This is a great start.

00:32:11.990 --> 00:32:14.807
I'm looking forward to the partnership,

00:32:14.807 --> 00:32:16.350
and the part of the partnership

00:32:16.350 --> 00:32:18.620
where we're having the real conversations

00:32:18.620 --> 00:32:20.940
about the things that our residents need.

00:32:20.940 --> 00:32:22.890
I'm looking for the uncomfortable parts

00:32:22.890 --> 00:32:25.000
of the conversations that will happen.

00:32:25.000 --> 00:32:28.250
Because any time I'm involved,
that come up sometime.

00:32:28.250 --> 00:32:30.390
About what our residents really need.

00:32:30.390 --> 00:32:33.270
I'm looking forward to the
part of the conversation

00:32:33.270 --> 00:32:35.220
where we actually have results about

00:32:35.220 --> 00:32:37.840
bringing people in this
city out of poverty.

00:32:37.840 --> 00:32:39.650
That's what I'm looking forward to.

00:32:39.650 --> 00:32:41.710
Because I think that's really important.

00:32:41.710 --> 00:32:44.980
And this agreement, it is
historic, as the Mayor said.

00:32:44.980 --> 00:32:48.530
It's historic, because
we actually had unions,

00:32:48.530 --> 00:32:50.100
elected officials,

00:32:50.100 --> 00:32:53.320
and community members,
all working together,

00:32:53.320 --> 00:32:57.180
over a number of years saying,
"Yale, please hear our cry."

00:32:57.180 --> 00:33:00.200
We have needs that we
really need help addressing.

00:33:00.200 --> 00:33:01.797
And Yale stepped up and said,

00:33:01.797 --> 00:33:04.150
"We're hearing you, we're listening."

00:33:04.150 --> 00:33:07.660
But a partnership is not over
once an agreement is signed.

00:33:07.660 --> 00:33:09.540
It goes far beyond that.

00:33:09.540 --> 00:33:10.760
So today is historic,

00:33:10.760 --> 00:33:12.680
and I'm actually really
proud to stand here.

00:33:12.680 --> 00:33:15.890
I don't come to many things
if I don't believe in it.

00:33:15.890 --> 00:33:17.390
But anything that I'm involved in,

00:33:17.390 --> 00:33:18.920
I won't take my foot off the gas.

00:33:18.920 --> 00:33:20.970
I won't stop pressing the real issues.

00:33:20.970 --> 00:33:22.700
I won't stop speaking for the residents

00:33:22.700 --> 00:33:25.470
that felt like they never
had a voice up until now.

00:33:25.470 --> 00:33:26.940
I'll continue doing that.

00:33:26.940 --> 00:33:30.730
And I know that Yale hear,
you know, they hear that call.

00:33:30.730 --> 00:33:32.670
I work with them in many
different capacities,

00:33:32.670 --> 00:33:34.890
and I'm always the same way, I might add,

00:33:34.890 --> 00:33:36.580
in all little spaces.

00:33:36.580 --> 00:33:39.360
But for me, in order to
really be celebrating,

00:33:39.360 --> 00:33:41.780
and we are celebrating,
because this is historic,

00:33:41.780 --> 00:33:43.770
this is great for our city.

00:33:43.770 --> 00:33:45.621
But the things that I really care about,

00:33:45.621 --> 00:33:47.030
because I grew up here, too.

00:33:47.030 --> 00:33:50.700
I am one of those
residents that felt like,

00:33:50.700 --> 00:33:52.270
you get what you get from Yale,

00:33:52.270 --> 00:33:54.010
and you can't get anything else.

00:33:54.010 --> 00:33:56.620
So, I'm standing here
today for all those people

00:33:56.620 --> 00:33:59.170
that didn't have faith
that a real partnership

00:33:59.170 --> 00:34:00.270
was gonna happen.

00:34:00.270 --> 00:34:03.000
So it's my responsibility
to make sure that happened.

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:05.820
And I won't stop, I just
wanna let yous know know.

00:34:05.820 --> 00:34:07.400
It will get uncomfortable,

00:34:07.400 --> 00:34:08.980
but we will get result.

00:34:08.980 --> 00:34:10.042
Thank you.

00:34:10.042 --> 00:34:12.959
(group applauding)

00:34:28.360 --> 00:34:29.390
- I think there's a lot of us in this room

00:34:29.390 --> 00:34:32.160
that can testify to that discomfort

00:34:32.160 --> 00:34:33.140
that sometimes we feel

00:34:33.140 --> 00:34:35.720
when talking to President Walker-Myers.

00:34:35.720 --> 00:34:38.230
Dean Charles? Would you mind coming up

00:34:38.230 --> 00:34:40.568
and saying a few words about the center?

00:34:40.568 --> 00:34:43.485
(group applauding)

00:34:46.080 --> 00:34:47.430
- I'm delighted to be here.

00:34:49.639 --> 00:34:51.560
I am grateful to have been included

00:34:52.402 --> 00:34:53.852
in the team on the Yale side.

00:34:55.000 --> 00:34:56.480
And even more grateful,

00:34:56.480 --> 00:34:59.060
that over the course of
the last several months,

00:34:59.060 --> 00:35:02.090
we on the Yale side, and I
speak for myself, personally.

00:35:02.090 --> 00:35:05.800
Have gotten a chance to
learn, to meet, to know,

00:35:05.800 --> 00:35:10.083
to befriend our partners
on the New Haven side.

00:35:11.160 --> 00:35:13.540
I say partners, because
it is my conviction,

00:35:13.540 --> 00:35:16.330
and I believe the view of the Yale side,

00:35:16.330 --> 00:35:18.130
if you call it that, more generally,

00:35:19.999 --> 00:35:22.990
that Yale's thriving is tied
up with New Haven's success,

00:35:22.990 --> 00:35:23.823
and vice-versa.

00:35:25.640 --> 00:35:28.460
I'm stimulated by where
we have reached today,

00:35:28.460 --> 00:35:30.170
the point we have reached.

00:35:30.170 --> 00:35:32.833
During the course of
the last several months,

00:35:34.520 --> 00:35:36.020
various things occurred to me.

00:35:37.010 --> 00:35:40.060
I, for example, learned about activities

00:35:40.060 --> 00:35:42.440
in which Yale is already engaged.

00:35:42.440 --> 00:35:44.610
And has been engaged for many years,

00:35:44.610 --> 00:35:46.610
scattered across the University.

00:35:46.610 --> 00:35:48.390
We just saw the music program,

00:35:48.390 --> 00:35:50.830
about which, I previously knew nothing.

00:35:50.830 --> 00:35:52.230
Their similar opportunities,

00:35:54.270 --> 00:35:55.910
or activities.

00:35:55.910 --> 00:35:58.340
Whereas Yale has been
engaged in activities

00:35:58.340 --> 00:36:02.400
in this university-wide fashion.

00:36:02.400 --> 00:36:06.690
Any honest assessment of the City's needs,

00:36:06.690 --> 00:36:08.243
in this historical moment,

00:36:09.130 --> 00:36:11.400
made clear, it seems to me,

00:36:11.400 --> 00:36:15.210
that there is opportunity and space.

00:36:15.210 --> 00:36:17.980
Opportunity and space for activities

00:36:17.980 --> 00:36:20.153
that foster inclusive growth.

00:36:21.820 --> 00:36:23.960
What is meant by that term?

00:36:23.960 --> 00:36:25.210
What is inclusive growth?

00:36:26.690 --> 00:36:29.490
Obviously, there's a part of
it that has allowed growth.

00:36:30.570 --> 00:36:33.500
By growth, I think, we jointly mean,

00:36:33.500 --> 00:36:38.190
vibrant and robust, and
thriving economic activity.

00:36:38.190 --> 00:36:39.840
Yes?

00:36:39.840 --> 00:36:41.300
But I think, jointly,

00:36:41.300 --> 00:36:43.163
we don't merely mean that.

00:36:44.040 --> 00:36:47.053
We mean thriving, robust
economic activity,

00:36:48.332 --> 00:36:51.460
that promotes health
and safety, for example.

00:36:51.460 --> 00:36:53.820
The sustainable, for example.

00:36:53.820 --> 00:36:55.260
Yes?

00:36:55.260 --> 00:36:57.060
But even more than that,

00:36:57.060 --> 00:37:00.290
the reason we include, as a group,

00:37:00.290 --> 00:37:03.660
the inclusive piece, is that
we are jointly-committed

00:37:03.660 --> 00:37:04.563
to the idea,

00:37:05.420 --> 00:37:08.123
that such growth, as it
occurs in our community,

00:37:09.280 --> 00:37:11.710
the benefits of that growth,

00:37:11.710 --> 00:37:13.550
must be diffusely spread

00:37:13.550 --> 00:37:16.100
across our community's neighborhoods,

00:37:16.100 --> 00:37:17.600
our community's racial groups.

00:37:18.660 --> 00:37:22.373
It must benefit whatever growth
occurs, all of New Haven.

00:37:24.100 --> 00:37:26.620
So when I think about what we might do

00:37:26.620 --> 00:37:28.873
on the inclusive growth side,

00:37:30.150 --> 00:37:30.983
I think

00:37:33.569 --> 00:37:35.700
there are multiple opportunities here

00:37:35.700 --> 00:37:39.680
to enlarge upon, ongoing programs,

00:37:39.680 --> 00:37:41.353
and to institute new ones.

00:37:42.380 --> 00:37:47.240
Enlarge upon ongoing programs,
and implement new ones.

00:37:47.240 --> 00:37:51.750
With the aim, guided by
the principle, I might say,

00:37:51.750 --> 00:37:53.250
that whatever we,

00:37:53.250 --> 00:37:57.173
new initiatives we start,
have the following features:

00:37:58.530 --> 00:38:00.180
That they be truly collaborative,

00:38:01.470 --> 00:38:03.120
truly collaborative.

00:38:03.120 --> 00:38:05.123
That they be genuinely respectful.

00:38:06.050 --> 00:38:09.300
Which is to say, that
whereas the Yale team might,

00:38:09.300 --> 00:38:12.160
in a particular case, interact
with New Haven partners

00:38:12.160 --> 00:38:14.000
on a particular problem,

00:38:14.000 --> 00:38:16.230
we will not approach our conversation

00:38:16.230 --> 00:38:17.640
with the presumption that

00:38:17.640 --> 00:38:20.270
all the ideas rest on the Yale side.

00:38:20.270 --> 00:38:21.150
Yes?

00:38:21.150 --> 00:38:23.390
We believe there is much to be learned

00:38:23.390 --> 00:38:24.953
from genuine collaboration.

00:38:25.890 --> 00:38:29.370
Our collaboration must be respectful, yes?

00:38:29.370 --> 00:38:32.970
And the hope is that it
would be mutually beneficial.

00:38:32.970 --> 00:38:35.033
Both to Yale, and to New Haven.

00:38:36.650 --> 00:38:39.510
The opportunities for this
kind of inclusive activity,

00:38:39.510 --> 00:38:43.490
in my view, extend in
every department of Yale,

00:38:43.490 --> 00:38:45.163
every school, every program.

00:38:46.510 --> 00:38:48.880
I'm the Dean of the Yale
School of Management.

00:38:48.880 --> 00:38:51.880
And I speak to you as the
representative of a single school.

00:38:52.720 --> 00:38:54.460
Where other deans present,

00:38:54.460 --> 00:38:56.810
I'm confident that each
of them could point

00:38:56.810 --> 00:39:00.570
to some set of activities
ongoing, or contemplating, yes?

00:39:00.570 --> 00:39:02.913
That would advanced the
ends I just described.

00:39:04.270 --> 00:39:07.530
If I may take a moment
to speak about my school,

00:39:07.530 --> 00:39:10.430
and how I envision where it sits

00:39:10.430 --> 00:39:12.180
in the new center we are launching.

00:39:13.650 --> 00:39:15.850
I believe that the Yale
School of Management

00:39:17.234 --> 00:39:20.220
has an out-sized imperative,

00:39:20.220 --> 00:39:22.863
and similarly, out-sized opportunity,

00:39:23.820 --> 00:39:26.060
to profitably, and
meaningfully contribute,

00:39:26.060 --> 00:39:27.460
in the way I just described.

00:39:28.720 --> 00:39:31.210
Why this out-sized imperative
of what do I speak?

00:39:31.210 --> 00:39:32.733
What do I mean by that?

00:39:34.560 --> 00:39:36.110
Our imperative is out-sized,

00:39:36.110 --> 00:39:38.120
and our opportunity is out-sized

00:39:38.120 --> 00:39:40.070
because of the combination of

00:39:40.070 --> 00:39:42.420
our peculiar distinctive mission

00:39:42.420 --> 00:39:44.013
at the Yale School of Management.

00:39:44.860 --> 00:39:47.180
The special expertise interest

00:39:47.180 --> 00:39:49.220
and talents of our students,

00:39:49.220 --> 00:39:52.133
and the research and scholarly
focus of our faculty.

00:39:53.920 --> 00:39:56.260
A brief word about the mission.

00:39:56.260 --> 00:39:57.900
The Yale School of Management is the only

00:39:57.900 --> 00:39:59.570
business school in the country

00:39:59.570 --> 00:40:01.657
that is organized, run
a mission that says,

00:40:01.657 --> 00:40:05.480
"It seeks to train leaders
for business, and society."

00:40:05.480 --> 00:40:06.387
It says.

00:40:06.387 --> 00:40:07.417
"And society."

00:40:08.830 --> 00:40:10.453
If that is truly our motto,

00:40:11.430 --> 00:40:14.390
if that is truly our animating value,

00:40:14.390 --> 00:40:17.113
let's begin by trying
to help this society.

00:40:18.270 --> 00:40:19.820
The community in which we live.

00:40:21.050 --> 00:40:23.630
Where our neighbors and
friends are every day.

00:40:23.630 --> 00:40:25.993
Let's begin there, yes?

00:40:27.050 --> 00:40:29.690
The special motto and set
of values of the school

00:40:29.690 --> 00:40:32.660
is reflected in the kinds of students

00:40:32.660 --> 00:40:33.810
who come to the school.

00:40:34.700 --> 00:40:37.130
The kinds of students
who come to the school.

00:40:37.130 --> 00:40:38.870
Animated by,

00:40:38.870 --> 00:40:42.210
not only a desire to be
successful business people,

00:40:42.210 --> 00:40:44.900
but to transform the
world in a productive,

00:40:44.900 --> 00:40:46.940
useful, and meaningful way

00:40:46.940 --> 00:40:48.683
to benefit multiple stakeholders.

00:40:50.220 --> 00:40:53.320
The Mayor knows a bit of whereof I speak.

00:40:53.320 --> 00:40:55.800
Because some of our
students, and our graduates,

00:40:55.800 --> 00:40:59.443
go on to do things like
become Mayor of their city.

00:40:59.443 --> 00:41:00.350
(group laughing)

00:41:00.350 --> 00:41:01.200
Talk to me.

00:41:01.200 --> 00:41:03.800
Yeah? So the Mayor knows about that.

00:41:03.800 --> 00:41:06.240
So we have mission.

00:41:06.240 --> 00:41:08.460
We have the values of our students.

00:41:08.460 --> 00:41:10.920
Those students also possess certain kinds

00:41:10.920 --> 00:41:13.553
of human capital, expertise, and energy

00:41:13.553 --> 00:41:16.930
that will benefit our city and community.

00:41:16.930 --> 00:41:19.860
Our students can have internships here

00:41:19.860 --> 00:41:21.210
at the City.

00:41:21.210 --> 00:41:23.670
Our students can work on research projects

00:41:23.670 --> 00:41:25.390
imagined by the City team.

00:41:25.390 --> 00:41:28.310
Our students can engage and work with

00:41:28.310 --> 00:41:30.290
non-profit organizations,

00:41:30.290 --> 00:41:33.533
conversations that I have
already started as Dean.

00:41:35.870 --> 00:41:37.640
On the research side,

00:41:37.640 --> 00:41:40.200
some of the questions, the research ideas

00:41:40.200 --> 00:41:42.050
that animate our faculty,

00:41:42.050 --> 00:41:44.793
concern matters that are
interest to the City.

00:41:46.000 --> 00:41:48.750
We have experts in any number of fields.

00:41:48.750 --> 00:41:50.220
Operations,

00:41:50.220 --> 00:41:51.220
finance,

00:41:51.220 --> 00:41:52.430
accounting.

00:41:52.430 --> 00:41:56.340
Who's ideas would be of value to our city,

00:41:56.340 --> 00:41:58.730
as the growth we all imagine,

00:41:58.730 --> 00:42:01.870
and hope for occurs in the coming years.

00:42:01.870 --> 00:42:03.840
The non-profit sector.

00:42:03.840 --> 00:42:06.373
Launching entrepreneur activity.

00:42:07.470 --> 00:42:09.560
Social impact investing.

00:42:09.560 --> 00:42:10.393
I can go on.

00:42:11.680 --> 00:42:13.940
The precise details of all that will occur

00:42:13.940 --> 00:42:18.763
under the umbrella of our new
initiative are not yet clear.

00:42:19.740 --> 00:42:20.790
But I can assure you,

00:42:21.670 --> 00:42:23.613
that I, and we, collectively,

00:42:24.490 --> 00:42:28.520
embark upon that mission
with great enthusiasm.

00:42:28.520 --> 00:42:30.960
And I personally have profound confidence,

00:42:30.960 --> 00:42:31.793
that although we'll have

00:42:31.793 --> 00:42:33.490
an uncomfortable
conversation here and there.

00:42:33.490 --> 00:42:34.404
I'm cool with that.

00:42:34.404 --> 00:42:35.283
(group laughing)

00:42:35.283 --> 00:42:36.940
I'm cool with that.

00:42:36.940 --> 00:42:41.690
That our success is assured
if we bring to that effort,

00:42:41.690 --> 00:42:43.910
the effort of collaboration,

00:42:43.910 --> 00:42:46.070
friendship, openness,

00:42:46.070 --> 00:42:49.000
and energy, will be brought
to our conversations

00:42:49.000 --> 00:42:50.690
over the last year.

00:42:50.690 --> 00:42:52.320
Thank you very much, Mayor.

00:42:52.320 --> 00:42:55.237
(group applauding)

00:43:00.470 --> 00:43:02.110
- So one of the speakers
stole my run of show,

00:43:02.110 --> 00:43:05.913
but I'm pretty sure we are
at our last speaker here.

00:43:07.339 --> 00:43:08.960
And when you think about
over this past two years,

00:43:08.960 --> 00:43:11.720
what we've accomplished as
a city is quite remarkable.

00:43:11.720 --> 00:43:14.870
Senator Looney, your leadership,

00:43:14.870 --> 00:43:17.580
I always talk about how I sat down

00:43:17.580 --> 00:43:21.020
at the Greek Olive with
you, just about two years,

00:43:21.020 --> 00:43:22.670
and you said, "I have this idea."

00:43:24.294 --> 00:43:26.560
This has dramatically benefited New Haven.

00:43:26.560 --> 00:43:29.240
But, what we don't often
talk about in New Haven,

00:43:29.240 --> 00:43:32.620
is how many other towns
and cities across our state

00:43:32.620 --> 00:43:35.240
have received a significant increase

00:43:35.240 --> 00:43:37.840
in the investment, because
of Senator Looney's work.

00:43:38.760 --> 00:43:43.760
Those cities and towns are ones of need.

00:43:43.760 --> 00:43:46.060
So the impact that you had
on our city is incredible,

00:43:46.060 --> 00:43:47.750
with the impact on our state, as well.

00:43:47.750 --> 00:43:50.418
So Senator Looney, maybe
you can finish us off here.

00:43:50.418 --> 00:43:51.260
- Thank you, Mayor.

00:43:51.260 --> 00:43:54.177
(group applauding)

00:44:00.067 --> 00:44:01.540
- Thank you so much, Mayor.

00:44:01.540 --> 00:44:05.740
This is truly a historic
day with this announcement

00:44:05.740 --> 00:44:08.190
between the City of New
Haven, and Yale University.

00:44:08.190 --> 00:44:10.460
I wanna congratulate Mayor Elicker,

00:44:10.460 --> 00:44:12.050
President Salovey,

00:44:12.050 --> 00:44:13.343
their negotiating teams,

00:44:14.998 --> 00:44:17.580
certainly Dean Charles, Mr.
Callahan, on the Yale side.

00:44:17.580 --> 00:44:20.630
Henry Fernandez, the visionary
leader on the City side.

00:44:20.630 --> 00:44:23.270
Also like to give a shout
out to my dear friend

00:44:23.270 --> 00:44:25.020
of 35 years, Sue Weisselberg,

00:44:25.020 --> 00:44:27.250
who was on the City team.

00:44:27.250 --> 00:44:29.082
And Sue...
(group applauding)

00:44:29.082 --> 00:44:32.138
As many know...
(group applauding)

00:44:32.138 --> 00:44:33.980
A number of years ago,
she served as Council,

00:44:33.980 --> 00:44:37.520
very effectively as Council
to our House Democratic Caucus

00:44:37.520 --> 00:44:38.470
in the General Assembly,

00:44:38.470 --> 00:44:42.390
and then, also directed the
school construction program

00:44:42.390 --> 00:44:44.130
for the City of New Haven for many years,

00:44:44.130 --> 00:44:46.560
which also, set a mark for the state,

00:44:46.560 --> 00:44:49.961
in which a 1.5 billion program

00:44:49.961 --> 00:44:53.850
to revolutionize New Haven's
physical plan of it's schools.

00:44:53.850 --> 00:44:56.200
In which about 1.2 billion was paid

00:44:56.200 --> 00:44:58.723
by the State of Connecticut,
in that partnership.

00:44:59.836 --> 00:45:02.453
And her management of that
inspired great confidence.

00:45:03.544 --> 00:45:05.340
But what we see here today
is, has been discussed,

00:45:05.340 --> 00:45:07.803
is a new day.

00:45:09.489 --> 00:45:12.090
And one of the things that
I think is most important,

00:45:12.090 --> 00:45:14.493
early in the 2021 session,

00:45:15.370 --> 00:45:17.620
President Salovey said
to me in a conversation,

00:45:17.620 --> 00:45:21.020
if the State of Connecticut
is willing to enhance

00:45:21.020 --> 00:45:23.530
it's commitment to the City of New Haven,

00:45:23.530 --> 00:45:27.230
Yale will certainly respond
in kind, and do the same.

00:45:27.230 --> 00:45:28.930
The State of Connecticut has done that.

00:45:28.930 --> 00:45:32.210
And today, Yale announces
that it is also doing that.

00:45:32.210 --> 00:45:35.420
And I think that that
is a meeting of promises

00:45:35.420 --> 00:45:36.340
and commitments,

00:45:36.340 --> 00:45:39.370
and aspirations on both sides.

00:45:39.370 --> 00:45:41.310
President Salovey is
someone that I've know

00:45:41.310 --> 00:45:42.580
and admired for a long time.

00:45:42.580 --> 00:45:44.370
I think he has done a superb
job as Yale President.

00:45:44.370 --> 00:45:46.440
We developed a close,
working relationship.

00:45:46.440 --> 00:45:49.620
But years, in fact, a couple
of decades before that,

00:45:49.620 --> 00:45:51.300
I worked closely as a board member,

00:45:51.300 --> 00:45:53.370
the Fair Haven Community Health Center,

00:45:53.370 --> 00:45:54.300
with his wife,

00:45:54.300 --> 00:45:56.820
wonderfully public-spirited
and brilliant woman,

00:45:56.820 --> 00:46:00.450
Marta Moret, so please
say hello to Marta for me.

00:46:00.450 --> 00:46:03.500
And at the time, they lived
within the service area

00:46:03.500 --> 00:46:05.007
of the Fair Haven Health Center.

00:46:05.007 --> 00:46:06.823
The lived in Fair Haven.

00:46:08.017 --> 00:46:09.710
So Peter Salovey knows
the view of New Haven

00:46:09.710 --> 00:46:11.770
from elsewhere than Hillhouse Avenue.

00:46:11.770 --> 00:46:13.360
Because he has lived that, too.

00:46:13.360 --> 00:46:15.190
So, I wanted to thank him for that.

00:46:15.190 --> 00:46:17.930
Again, the Pilot program that we were

00:46:17.930 --> 00:46:22.160
able to reconfigure, in this session,

00:46:22.160 --> 00:46:25.220
was something that had been
percolating for a while,

00:46:25.220 --> 00:46:26.470
as Mayor Elicker and I strategized to bat,

00:46:26.470 --> 00:46:29.470
a couple of years ago at the
Greek Olive, which is defunct,

00:46:29.470 --> 00:46:31.463
but the program, of course, is not.

00:46:31.463 --> 00:46:33.842
(group laughing)

00:46:33.842 --> 00:46:34.960
And what it amounted to,

00:46:34.960 --> 00:46:36.580
was a recognition that there are,

00:46:36.580 --> 00:46:40.970
the Pilot needed a more
expanded constituency.

00:46:40.970 --> 00:46:42.070
And the way to do that,

00:46:42.070 --> 00:46:45.210
was to involve more towns
in Pilot eligibility.

00:46:45.210 --> 00:46:46.700
And the approach that we found to do that,

00:46:46.700 --> 00:46:50.363
was to look at equalized
net grand list per capita.

00:46:51.273 --> 00:46:53.230
Now there are a large number
of relatively poor communities

00:46:53.230 --> 00:46:55.560
in the state, notably
in Eastern Connecticut,

00:46:55.560 --> 00:46:56.510
that are struggling,

00:46:56.510 --> 00:46:58.710
even though they many not have
a lot of tax exempt property,

00:46:58.710 --> 00:47:00.340
or Pilot-eligible property.

00:47:00.340 --> 00:47:01.940
But, to the extent that they do,

00:47:02.900 --> 00:47:05.120
if they have a net grand list per capita

00:47:05.120 --> 00:47:09.120
of under $100,000, as of
course, all the major cities do,

00:47:09.120 --> 00:47:11.900
they will get 50%
reimbursement under Pilot

00:47:11.900 --> 00:47:14.670
for the tax-exempt college and hospital,

00:47:14.670 --> 00:47:16.420
and also state property.

00:47:16.420 --> 00:47:18.120
For those in the next tier,

00:47:18.120 --> 00:47:21.760
between $100,000 and $200,000
of tax-exempt property,

00:47:21.760 --> 00:47:23.470
those communities will get 40%.

00:47:23.470 --> 00:47:25.080
And then, those over $200,000

00:47:25.080 --> 00:47:27.960
and net grand list per
capita, will get 30%.

00:47:27.960 --> 00:47:30.620
Now again, every tier is
above the current level

00:47:30.620 --> 00:47:34.069
of reimbursement, which is about 25%.

00:47:34.069 --> 00:47:35.220
So one of the reasons
we were able to develop

00:47:35.220 --> 00:47:36.520
broad-based consensus on this,

00:47:36.520 --> 00:47:39.730
that even the towns in the lowest tier,

00:47:39.730 --> 00:47:41.140
were gonna get a significant increase

00:47:41.140 --> 00:47:44.023
over what Pilot had been most recently.

00:47:44.989 --> 00:47:47.320
So, we believe that this
will be transformative.

00:47:47.320 --> 00:47:50.660
And coupled with the
increase in funding for Yale,

00:47:50.660 --> 00:47:52.050
over the life of this program,

00:47:52.050 --> 00:47:54.090
especially, Yale's commitment to continue

00:47:54.090 --> 00:47:56.740
to pay taxes on properties newly taken off

00:47:56.740 --> 00:47:59.040
the tax rolls for a period of time.

00:47:59.040 --> 00:48:01.530
This is something that is going to result

00:48:01.530 --> 00:48:03.650
in what we hoped it would do.

00:48:03.650 --> 00:48:06.790
And I also wanna especially
thank my colleagues on this,

00:48:06.790 --> 00:48:09.420
especially Representative Toni Walker,

00:48:09.420 --> 00:48:11.340
of New Haven, who is, of course,

00:48:11.340 --> 00:48:14.030
the House Chair of the General Assembly's

00:48:14.030 --> 00:48:15.550
Appropriations Committee.

00:48:15.550 --> 00:48:19.000
In that position, she was
critical in this enterprise.

00:48:19.000 --> 00:48:20.950
And also, the Speaker of
the House, Matt Ritter,

00:48:20.950 --> 00:48:23.520
who represents Hartford,
and his support for this,

00:48:23.520 --> 00:48:24.770
of course, was critical.

00:48:24.770 --> 00:48:27.070
And his city, of course,
is a major beneficiary

00:48:27.070 --> 00:48:30.218
of the reconfigured Pilot, as well.

00:48:30.218 --> 00:48:32.900
So again, I'm glad we were
able to come to this day.

00:48:32.900 --> 00:48:36.150
I had confidence from the
very beginning that we would.

00:48:36.150 --> 00:48:38.180
Because of the people involved,

00:48:38.180 --> 00:48:40.630
and the honesty, and the earnestness,

00:48:40.630 --> 00:48:42.870
and the solidity of their commitment.

00:48:42.870 --> 00:48:44.630
So I think we have a great
deal to celebrate today.

00:48:44.630 --> 00:48:46.026
Thank you, all.

00:48:46.026 --> 00:48:48.943
(group applauding)

